  "prompt_text": "The figure is a simplified diagram of the microcirculation, showing how blood moves from the arterial side to the venous side through progressively smaller vessels. On the left, a large red artery branches into smaller arterioles, which then feed a broad network of capillaries in the center. This capillary bed is drawn as a dense mesh of tiny interconnected vessels, emphasizing it as the main exchange region between the two sides of the circulation.\n\nAcross the figure, the color gradually shifts from warm red on the artery side to cooler blue on the vein side, visually indicating the transition from oxygen-rich to oxygen-poor blood. After passing through the capillary network, blood collects into venules and then drains into a larger blue vein on the right. The vessel diameters also change in a clear pattern: large artery, smaller arterioles, very fine capillaries, then small venules and a larger vein.\n\nOverall, the image conveys the hierarchical branching and merging structure of the vascular system and highlights the capillary network as the bridge between arterial delivery and venous return.",
